// Tailscale ACL policy for tailnet <tailnet>.ts.net — the DURABLE layer that scopes
// the mini PC (debian) to its owner.
//
// The tailnet currently has NO acls block, which means default allow-all: every node
// reaches every node. That is why <nas-owner-email>'s devices (the NAS + an iPhone)
// can reach every no-auth port on debian. Adding this `acls` list switches the tailnet
// to default-deny, so ONLY the flows listed below are allowed.
//
// Only two users exist and the only homelab flows are owner-initiated (debian -> NAS
// for xfer/Jellyfin/ssh, and the Mac -> everything), so this policy preserves every
// real flow while denying the NAS owner -> the owner's devices (i.e. -> debian).
//
// APPLY VIA THE TAILSCALE ADMIN CONSOLE (Access Controls). The editor validates and
// previews before saving, and you keep console access even if a rule is wrong — so a
// mistake here does not lock you out of the tailnet, only of node-to-node traffic.
//
// Replace <owner-email> and <nas-owner-email> with the two Tailscale logins before
// pasting; the real addresses are not committed.
{
  "acls": [
    // The owner's own devices (Mac, debian) may reach the entire tailnet.
    { "action": "accept", "src": ["<owner-email>"], "dst": ["*:*"] },

    // The other user's devices may talk amongst themselves...
    { "action": "accept", "src": ["<nas-owner-email>"], "dst": ["<nas-owner-email>:*"] },

    // ...but nothing grants <nas-owner-email> -> <owner-email>, so the
    // NAS and iPhone can no longer reach debian's ports. This is the whole point.
  ],

  // Keep node ownership pinned so tags/owners are unambiguous if you extend this later.
  "tagOwners": {},
}
